{"id":"leetcode-349","version":1,"status":"approved","title":"Intersection of Two Arrays","summary":"Given two integer arrays, nums1 and nums2, return an array containing the unique elements that are present in both arrays.","statement":"Given two integer arrays, nums1 and nums2, return an array containing the unique elements that are present in both arrays. The order of elements in the result does not matter.","constraints":["1 <= len(nums1), len(nums2) <= 1000; 0 <= every element <= 1000.","Return each shared value exactly once, in any order. Repeated input occurrences do not repeat the output."],"topics":["arrays","hash-maps"],"prerequisites":[],"signature":{"name":"unique_intersection","parameters":[{"name":"nums1","annotation":"list[int]","adapter":"raw","source_parameter":null},{"name":"nums2","annotation":"list[int]","adapter":"raw","source_parameter":null}],"returns":"list[int]","return_adapter":"raw","node_policy":"values","result_mode":"return","mutated_parameter":null,"immutable_parameters":[]},"starter_code":"def unique_intersection(nums1: list[int], nums2: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    # Write your solution here.\n    pass\n","reference_solution":"def unique_intersection(nums1, nums2):\n    return list(set(nums1) & set(nums2))\n","brute_force_solution":null,"known_wrong_solutions":["def unique_intersection(nums1, nums2):\n    return list(set(nums1) | set(nums2))","def unique_intersection(nums1, nums2):\n    return [x for x in nums1 if x in nums2]"],"target_time_complexity":"O(n + m) expected","target_space_complexity":"O(n + m)","estimated_minutes":20,"rubric":{"algorithmic_insight":1,"prerequisites":1,"implementation":1,"performance_pressure":0,"edge_cases":0},"hints":["Membership and frequency are different contracts.","Use sets for both inputs and retain only common values.","Build A=set(nums1), B=set(nums2); return list(A & B).","Set intersection discards repeated occurrences and keeps exactly shared values."],"editorial":"Represent each input by its distinct values. Their intersection contains a value if and only if it occurs in both inputs. Any output order is allowed, but duplicate outputs are not. The independent oracle scans sorted distinct left values using membership in the original right list.","tests":[{"id":"lc-349-1","arguments":[[1,2,2,1],[2,2]],"expected":[2],"comparator":"unordered","visible":true,"explanation":"Only 2 occurs in both arrays. Return [2], not [2,2].","expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-349-2","arguments":[[4,9,5],[9,4,9,8,4]],"expected":[4,9],"comparator":"unordered","visible":true,"explanation":"The shared values are 4 and 9; either [4,9] or [9,4] is valid.","expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-349-3","arguments":[[0],[0]],"expected":[0],"comparator":"unordered","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-349-4","arguments":[[0],[1]],"expected":[],"comparator":"unordered","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-349-5","arguments":[[1],[1,1]],"expected":[1],"comparator":"unordered","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-349-6","arguments":[[1,1],[1]],"expected":[1],"comparator":"unordered","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-349-7","arguments":[[2,2],[2,2,2]],"expected":[2],"comparator":"unordered","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-349-8","arguments":[[1,2],[2,1]],"expected":[1,2],"comparator":"unordered","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-349-9","arguments":[[0,1000],[1000]],"expected":[1000],"comparator":"unordered","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-349-10","arguments":[[3,4],[5,6]],"expected":[],"comparator":"unordered","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-349-11","arguments":[[1,2,3],[3,2]],"expected":[2,3],"comparator":"unordered","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{